HTML to APK: my workflow notes

Build an Android APK from a plain HTML/CSS/JS app without installing Android Studio. Capacitor wraps the web app, GitHub Actions builds the APK in the cloud.

First project: Notepad (com.overman.notepad), repo github.com/Overman0101/notepad-app.

How it fits together
Write the app as a single index.html (HTML, CSS, JS inside).
Capacitor wraps it in an Android project (the android folder).
Push to GitHub. A workflow file tells GitHub to build the APK.
Download the APK from the Actions tab, copy it to the phone, install.
One-time setup (already done on this PC)
Node.js installed (v24).
Git installed (git-scm.com).
GitHub account.
Git identity set:
  git config --global user.name "Overman0101"
  git config --global user.email "my-github-email"
New project, step by step

Run all commands in the project folder, for example C:\Users\cp360admin\my-app.

Create the folder and a www subfolder, then put index.html inside www.
   mkdir www
Set up the project and Capacitor. Keep the three Capacitor versions identical.
   npm init -y
   npm install @capacitor/core @capacitor/cli @capacitor/android
   npx cap init "App Name" com.overman.appname --web-dir=www
   npx cap add android
   npx cap sync

Every line should show a green check mark. 3. Create .gitignore containing one line:

   node_modules
Create .github\workflows\build-apk.yml (contents below).
Make an empty repo on github.com (no README), then:
   git init
   git add .
   git commit -m "First version"
   git branch -M main
   git remote add origin https://github.com/Overman0101/REPO-NAME.git
   git push -u origin main
On GitHub open Actions, wait for the green check (3 to 5 minutes), open the run, download the artifact (zip), unzip, copy app-debug.apk to the phone, tap to install, allow unknown sources.
Updating an existing app
Edit www\index.html.
Push:
   git add .
   git commit -m "What I changed"
   git push
A new APK builds automatically. Download it from Actions again. Installing over the old one keeps its notes as long as the app ID is the same.
Workflow file: .github/workflows/build-apk.yml
yaml
name: Build APK

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 24
          cache: npm

      - uses: actions/setup-java@v4
        with:
          distribution: temurin
          java-version: 21

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Copy web files into the Android project
        run: npx cap sync android

      - name: Build debug APK
        working-directory: android
        run: |
          chmod +x gradlew
          ./gradlew assembleDebug

      - name: Upload APK
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: app-debug-apk
          path: android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
Things that tripped me up (and the fixes)
npm cap vs npx cap: it's npx cap. npm cap doesn't exist.
"web assets directory must contain an index.html": index.html has to be inside www, not the project root.
npx cap open android fails: it needs Android Studio. Not needed here, since the cloud builds the APK.
npx cap android is not a command: the real ones are init, add android, sync, open android.
Capacitor versions must match: core, cli, and android packages on the same version. Check with npm ls @capacitor/core @capacitor/cli @capacitor/android.
Don't run npm audit fix --force: it bumped one package and caused a version mismatch. The audit warnings are in build tooling, not the APK.
"Author identity unknown" on commit: set user.name and user.email (see setup), then commit again.
src refspec main does not match any: no commit exists yet. Fix the commit first, then push.
"LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings: harmless, ignore.
App ID is permanent: com.overman.notepad identifies the app on the phone. Pick it before the first install.
A folder getting recreated wipes the setup: check with dir that android, www, node_modules, package.json, and capacitor.config.json exist before committing.
Good to know
The APK is a debug build: fine for personal use and sideloading. Publishing to the Play Store needs a signed release build.
localStorage works inside the Capacitor WebView, so data persists on the phone.
The Android back button works with the web app through history.pushState.
Changing the app name, icon, or splash screen is a later step (the @capacitor/assets tool can generate icons from one image).
